<p>I wrote something down recently, and later I couldn't find it. If you know me, you know why; I put my notes into too many different apps.</p>
<p>Once in a while I declare loudly that &quot;I'm using only this app, from now on! I mean it!&quot; and then change my mind a month later. It's just the way I am. This most recent failure was the last straw and finally forced me to deal with it properly.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>Instead of fixing myself, I built a software crutch. I spent today vibe-coding an indexer and search engine for all of my stuff. Here's the initial version of a web UI:</p>
<p><img src="https://baty.net/img/2026/20261016-archive-search-stats.png" alt="" /></p>
<p>I also hacked together a TUI and an Emacs package. Those access the data via Tailscale SSH so I don't need to worry about installing things on other machines. Just the wrapper script.</p>
<p>Here's the list of sources being indexed (so far):</p>
<pre><code>./archive index
baty.net: 1272 items (0.9s)
